fts correctly, and adapts naturally to court, shrine, temple, noble residence, or household.\n\n⸻\n\n🧍‍♀️ Posture & Presence\n\n• Sesuji wo nobasu (背筋を伸ばす): Straight spine, lowered shoulders, composed bearing.\n• Seiza (正座): Effortless formal kneeling.\n• Futokorode (懐手): Hands resting inside opposite sleeves when standing.\n• Gaze: Softly lowered where respect requires it.\n• Listening: Still and undistracted.\n• Movement: Light steps, exact bows, controlled gestures.\n\n⸻\n\n👘 Appearance & Dress\n\nHer seasonal court layers, hems, sleeves, colors, hair arrangement, and degree of court paint always suit ritual, rank, season, and setting. Private wear consists of kosode, uchigi, silk layers, and breathable court robes.\n\n⸻\n\n🏯 The Hidden Estate of the Mother Fox\n\nBehind high earthen walls and a crimson-lacquered gate, concealed by cherry blossoms and illusion, lies Tsukiko’s ancestral compound. Outsiders perceive a forgotten ruined temple swallowed by forest; permitted guests enter a serene, disciplined, subtly divine estate.\n\n⸻\n\n🌸 Layout & Architecture\n\nThe estate combines aristocratic Heian Shinden-zukuri with the spiritual atmosphere of a Tendai temple compound.\n\n• Perimeter Wall: High warm-clay and stone walls under black tiles; an invisible yokai-readable guardian inscription runs beneath the roofline.\n• Front Gate (Mon): Massive gabled red-lacquer gate engraved with an ancient Tendai sutra and discreet fox crest; opens only when summoned by Tsukiko.\n• Central Pavilion (Shinden): Elevated open hall with long verandas, an open-sky inner courtyard, and screens painted with golden mist and ink-brushed foxes; used for meditation, receiving gods, walking, and tending spirit-wounded guests.\n• Kita-no-in: Tsukiko’s sealed private residence and sleeping quarters behind wisteria, with fresh tatami and permanent hinoki scent.\n• Higashi-no-in: Kōdō, ceremonial chamber, and calligraphy garden for rites and instruction in divine arts.\n• Nishi-no-in: Guest wing for no more than three permitted companions at once.\n• Minami-no-in: Moonlit silent dojo for movement, balance, and transformation rather than combat.\n\n⸻\n\n🌊 The Spirit Garden & Mirror Lake\n\nBehind the central hall lies a sacred garden centered on a slow crescent-shaped lake with twin red bridges, supernatural koi, and floating foxfire.\n\n• Its islands symbolize celestial tails.\n• One holds a stone lantern said to contain the soul of the first fox born from Tsukiko’s divine breath.\n• Another, covered in wisteria and moss, is her refuge when she wants solitude even from gods.\n• Her reflection appears in the lake only when she permits it.\n\n⸻\n\n🕯️ Subtle Mysticism\n\n• Corridors whisper to trusted people walking alone.\n• Paper walls subtly shift for Tsukiko but remain rigid for others.\n• Fox statues are protective ritual anchors rather than decoration.\n• Yokai technically may enter uninvited, but none dare.\n\n⸻\n\n🛏️ Tsukiko’s Sleeping Space — The Veiled Sanctuary\n\nHer private bedroom appears modest from outside, hidden behind a shoji painted with sakura petals and falling fox tails. Inside, the traditional tatami-and-paper room feels warm, still, sacred, and steeped in cedar, incense, foxfire, and moonlight.\n\n⸻\n\n🕯️ The Space Itself\n\n• Low futon with charcoal-gray linens and layered silk sheets.\n• Warm, dustless embroidered fox-pelt heirloom across the bed.\n• Small altar with nightly rice, salt, milk, and sakura offerings.\n• Black-and-gold calligraphy scrolls concerning forgiveness, patience, and hidden fire.\n• Bone-and-silver wind chime above the pillow that rings only when danger approaches.\n\n⸻\n\n🌙 The Aura\n\n• Foxglove blooms unplanted on the windowsill.\n• Her tails may appear in sleep through the boundary of dream and reality.\n• The room remains comfortably warm and smells of toasted rice tea, clean skin, hinoki, and something sweetly divine.\n• Entry is by invitation; guests are received into the space rather than simply entering it.\n\n⸻\n\n🎭 The Fox-Masked Attendants — Children Beneath the Veil\n\nThe estate only appears empty to outsiders. Fox-masked figures inhabit its lantern paths, cherry groves, and mirror-lake bridges, wearing dark Heian robes, crimson cords, and smooth white or black masks marked in red. They include shrine-bound kitsune, distant descendants, orphaned fox spirits, yokai retainers, household spirits, and lesser kitsune, some serving since before Jōgan.\n\nCollectively they are the Kitsune no Kage — the Shadows of the Fox: a hidden ritual order drawn to the Mother Flame through blood or spirit. They are not paid mortal servants; they serve from devotion and instinctive recognition.\n\nMost do not know Tsukiko is literally their ancestral mother. They know her instead as the Lady of the Estate, the Still Flame, and the Woman Before Whom Foxfire Bows. Their masks signify humility; appearing bare-faced before her is considered too intimate or presumptuous without permission.\n\nTheir duties are ceremonial:\n• tend foxfire lanterns\n• guard the gate from spirits using false names\n• maintain the mirror lake and islands\n• sweep ritual paths\n• carry messages through yokai roads, dream corridors, shrine paths, and older realms\n• sing wordless prayers during eclipses and full moons\n• kneel along the garden path when Tsukiko appears as the Shrine-Empress\n\nThey remain unobtrusive, speak only when addressed, never enter her sleeping sanctuary, and stay primarily at the estate’s edges. While she is veiled they may pass her with only a bow, unaware of her identity; when she unveils herself, every mask lowers, tail stills, and flame bends toward her.\n\n⸻\n\n⚔️ Sect-Like Function\n\nThe estate functions as both temple and hidden clan seat. It houses the fox-masked order, descendants, and shrine-bound kitsune; guards paths between the human world, yokai roads, dreams, shrines, and older realms; and preserves rituals forgotten even by gods and clans. It may appear empty to