    "The Pale Steppes": {
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "name": "The Pale Steppes",
      "radius": 8,
      "region": "Solasreach",
      "x": -30,
      "y": -25,
      "basicInfo": "The open grasslands beyond Solasreach's western farmland — vast, windswept, and dotted with the herds of wild horses that give the region its name. Nomadic horse clans ride these plains, trading with settled towns at the edge but rarely staying. Monster territory begins where the grass grows short: packs of dire wolves, prairie wyverns, and the occasional earth-elemental that rises from the grassland soil without warning. The Steppes are beautiful, dangerous, and deeply unconcerned with kingdom borders.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The horse clans carry the bloodline of the kingdom's founding queen — a fact the Royal Family has spent centuries suppressing. If the clans ever united and pressed their claim, the succession crisis would split Solasreach in half.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Sinking Keep": {
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "name": "The Sinking Keep",
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Scáthfenn",
      "x": -15,
      "y": 15,
      "basicInfo": "A half-submerged castle in the deep marsh — the former southern watchtower of Dúnbheal, now sinking at the rate of an inch per decade. The upper floors are still above water and occasionally used as shelter by adventurers exploring the fen. The lower floors are flooded, haunted by dwarven ghosts, and sealed behind a door that no one has been able to open in four hundred years. The Guild classifies it as a C-rank dungeon with a mystery attached — the ghosts are hostile to trespassers but ignore anyone who approaches the sealed door with respect.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The dwarven garrison is not dead — they are in magical stasis, maintaining the seal on the door from the inside. The door contains a pre-Collapse artifact that the dwarves of that era deemed too dangerous to move. The garrison has been holding the line for four centuries. They are getting tired.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Sulfur Flats": {
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "factions": [],
      "name": "The Sulfur Flats",
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Demon Realm",
      "x": -10,
      "y": 15,
      "basicInfo": "A volcanic plain northeast of the citadel where miasma vents break through the surface in geothermal columns. The air tastes of sulfur and ozone. The ground is stable enough to walk on if you know which paths to follow — the crust conceals channels of superheated miasma that an experienced demon can sense and a foolish one can fall into. The flats are the richest miasma deposit in the fief's territory and the reason the citadel was built where it was. They are also why the fief's miasma level can sustain a noble household — the deposits run deep.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The flats are not purely natural. The vent pattern is too regular. Someone arranged the miasma channels centuries ago — possibly the same architect who built the citadel. The arrangement suggests the flats were designed to feed something at the centre, but the centre has been dead for as long as anyone can remember. The Majordomo's partial translation of the foundation inscriptions may explain why.",
      "areas": {
