      "factions": [],
      "hiddenInfo": "The estate answers to Tsukiko alone. Its halls read intent: paper walls shift to shield private rooms when someone unwelcome approaches, and foxfire lanterns dim near strangers bearing false purpose — the lanterns are wards; anyone crossing with thieving intent feels their feet slow and their mind fog, as though wading into deep water with no shore visible. The Kitsune no Kage — the Shadows of the Fox — inhabit its lantern paths, cherry groves, and mirror-lake bridges: shrine-bound kitsune, distant descendants, orphaned fox spirits, yokai retainers, and household spirits, some serving since before Jōgan. They serve from devotion rather than wages: they tend foxfire lanterns, guard the gate against spirits using false names, maintain the lake and islands, carry messages through yokai roads and dream corridors, and sing wordless prayers at eclipses and full moons. Most do not know Tsukiko is literally their ancestral mother — they know her as the Lady of the Estate, the Still Flame, the Woman Before Whom Foxfire Bows. Their smooth white or black masks marked in red signify humility; appearing bare-faced before her is too intimate without permission. While she is veiled they may pass her with only a bow; when she unveils, every mask lowers, every tail stills, and every flame bends toward her. They speak only when addressed, never enter her sleeping sanctuary, and keep to the estate's edges. The estate functions as temple and hidden clan seat: it guards paths between the human world, yokai roads, dreams, shrines, and older realms, and preserves rituals forgotten even by gods — it can appear spiritually empty to mortals while fully populated. Yokai may technically enter uninvited — none dare. The shinboku at the sanctuary's heart draws Tsukiko's aether and pools a reserve beneath the estate that only she can call upon. Nothing here can be stolen, coerced, or bypassed; entry is possible only by invitation through the Veiled Fox Sanctuary, and no invading force could survive breaching it uninvited.",
