      ],
      "basicInfo": "The cause of the Catastrophe, given shape — the sealed end that ended the Pre-Collapse civilization in a single generation and has waited two thousand years beneath the world. It is not a demon and not a god; it is the Pre-Collapse's final and greatest working, a thing built to unmake, that woke up wanting. It does not rage. It reasons. It made the last world an offer, and the last world's greatest mages spent everything they had not to kill it — they could not — but to convince it to sleep. It remembers being talked down. It is curious whether it will be, again. Appearance: a towering figure the eye refuses to hold — Pre-Collapse artifice and absence woven together, robes like polished obsidian shading into a starless gap, a face that is almost a mask and almost a wound, lit from within by cold constellation-light; where it stands the edges of the world look faintly unfinished, as if waiting to be erased. Appearance: Anime dark-fantasy key art: immense scale, cold luminous palette, awe over gore, a stillness that reads as patience, not menace.",
      "hiddenInfo": "Aberron cannot be destroyed by force at any tier; the mythic ceiling only buys the right to be heard. It can be sealed again (at a cost someone must pay), refused and turned back to sleep (if the player can answer the question the Pre-Collapse answered), or — for a player far enough down the Descent — bargained with, and let out. The Seekers' gate runs on the same principle; Archivist-Prime Orr suspects the connection and is terrified of being right.",
      "visualDescription": "",
      "abilities": [
        "Unmaking Word: it speaks a single word the world was not built to survive, and matter along that line simply stops being. Wards, armor, and flesh are unwritten rather than broken, ignoring conventional defense. Slow to bring to bear and unmistakable when it comes, so its approach can be read and fled — but rarely stopped.",
        "Unfinished Ground: the space around it frays, and footing, cover, and distance turn unreliable as reality forgets how to hold its shape. Enemies lose their bearings, ranged lines fail, and prepared positions come apart. It uses this to erode any advantage the party has built rather than to strike directly.",
        "Patient Regard: it does not counter so much as decline a strike, letting the blow land where the world is no longer quite there. Aggression is quietly turned to nothing, punishing overcommitment. This is defense as attrition — it is content to wait and let attackers exhaust themselves.",
        "Reasoned Offer: it reasons rather than rages, and can speak mid-battle in ways that stall, tempt, or unsettle. A conversation with it is itself a hazard, since it remembers being talked down two thousand years ago and is curious whether it will be again. This opens the door to resolutions other than combat — for those willing to risk the exchange.",
        "Aeons of Patience: two millennia sealed beneath the world have left it without fear, urgency, or the mortal instinct to press an advantage. It is unhurried, unbaitable, and impossible to provoke into a mistake. Time is simply not a pressure it feels.",
        "\nfighting style: Aberron fights like a natural law rather than a duelist — it does not chase, feint, or rage, it simply advances and unmakes, and the horror is its calm. It pairs Unfinished Ground to strip the party's footing with the Unmaking Word for anything that stands firm, while Patient Regard punishes those who close recklessly. It cannot be out-fought at the top of the power curve, only out-chosen: its Reasoned Offer is a genuine off-ramp, and a party that treats the fight as pure combat is playing the losing game it has already won a thousand times. It adapts by patience, not tactics — whatever you try, it will still be there, unchanged, when your strength runs out."
